# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from part1 import e, E

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Periods and gynae health
e("late_period","Late or missed period","late period,missed period,period late,period is late,no period,period hasnt come,irregular period,irregular periods,skipped a period,period not coming,amenorrhea",
"Periods can be late because of stress, illness, travel, weight change, intense exercise, hormones (like PCOS or thyroid), breastfeeding, perimenopause, or pregnancy.\n\n• If you've had unprotected sex, take a pregnancy test (most work from the first day of a missed period)\n• See a clinic if you miss three periods in a row, are not pregnant, or also have unusual symptoms\n• Track your cycle in HerHub to see patterns\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis.","period","pregnancy_test,pcos,anxiety")
e("cramps","Period pain and cramps","cramps,period pain,painful period,menstrual cramps,dysmenorrhea,period hurts,stomach pain period,back pain period",
"Period pain is common. What can help:\n• A hot water bottle or warm bath\n• Gentle movement or stretching, and rest\n• Water, and regular meals\n• Over-the-counter pain relief such as ibuprofen or paracetamol, if it's safe for you. Follow the label\n\nSee a healthcare professional if pain is severe, is getting worse, stops you from daily life, or doesn't respond to pain relief. It could be endometriosis or another condition.","period","endometriosis,heavy_period,pms")
e("heavy_period","Heavy periods","heavy period,heavy bleeding,soaking pads,flooding,clots,bleeding too much,period lasts long,long period,bleeding a lot,menorrhagia,prolonged bleeding",
"Seek care if you:\n• Soak a pad or tampon every hour for several hours\n• Pass clots bigger than a coin\n• Bleed for more than 7 days\n• Feel dizzy, faint or very tired (possible anaemia)\n\nCauses include fibroids, hormonal imbalance, polyps, and other conditions. Treatments are available. Ask your clinic. If you are pregnant or could be, heavy bleeding is urgent: go to a clinic or hospital.","period","fibroids,anaemia,miscarriage")
e("pms","PMS and PMDD","pms,pmdd,premenstrual,mood swings before period,irritable before period,period mood,bloating before period,period anxiety,cravings before period",
"PMS includes mood changes, bloating, tender breasts, tiredness and cravings in the week or two before a period.\n\n• Regular sleep and exercise help\n• Eat balanced meals, and cut back on caffeine and salt\n• Log symptoms in HerHub to see your pattern\n\nIf mood symptoms are severe (intense sadness, anger, hopelessness), it could be PMDD. Talk to a healthcare professional. Treatment can help. If you feel hopeless, call SADAG 0800 21 22 23.","period","cramps,anxiety,depression")
e("spotting","Spotting between periods","spotting,bleeding between periods,breakthrough bleeding,bleeding after sex,bleeding after intercourse,brown discharge,light bleeding",
"Light spotting can happen with ovulation, new contraception, stress or hormonal changes. See a clinic if it keeps happening, occurs after sex, after menopause, or comes with pain or heavy bleeding.\n\nIf you could be pregnant and have bleeding with one-sided pain, go to a clinic or hospital urgently (ectopic pregnancy can be dangerous).","period","ectopic,stis,pregnancy_test")
e("pcos","PCOS","pcos,polycystic,polycystic ovary,excess hair growth,irregular cycles acne,hirsutism,insulin resistance,cant lose weight irregular",
"PCOS (polycystic ovary syndrome) is a common hormone condition. Signs include irregular or missing periods, acne, extra facial or body hair, scalp hair thinning, and weight gain. It can affect fertility.\n\n• A clinic can check hormones and do an ultrasound\n• Healthy eating, movement and sleep help many people\n• Medication can regulate periods or help with symptoms or pregnancy\n\nIt's manageable. Ask your clinic or doctor.","period","late_period,fertility,anaemia")
e("endometriosis","Endometriosis","endometriosis,severe period pain,pain during sex,chronic pelvic pain,pelvic pain,pain with bowel movements period,painful sex",
"Endometriosis is when tissue similar to the womb lining grows elsewhere, causing severe period pain, pelvic pain, pain during sex, heavy bleeding, and sometimes fertility problems. It's often missed for years.\n\nIf pain disrupts your life, see a doctor or gynaecologist. Keep a symptom diary in HerHub to show them. Treatments include pain relief, hormonal treatment and sometimes surgery.","period","cramps,fertility,pcos")
e("fibroids","Fibroids and cysts","fibroids,ovarian cyst,cyst,lump in womb,uterine fibroid,pelvic pressure,swollen tummy",
"Fibroids are non-cancerous growths in the womb, and ovarian cysts are fluid-filled sacs. Many cause no symptoms, but some cause heavy periods, pelvic pressure, pain, frequent urination or bloating.\n\nSee a clinic or doctor for a check and ultrasound. Sudden severe pelvic pain, fainting or fever needs urgent care.","period","heavy_period,endometriosis")
e("discharge","Vaginal discharge, thrush and BV","discharge,vaginal discharge,thrush,yeast infection,bv,bacterial vaginosis,itching down there,smelly discharge,vaginal itch,vagina smells,burning down there",
"Some discharge is normal and changes with your cycle. See a clinic if you notice:\n• Strong or fishy smell\n• Green, yellow or grey discharge\n• Itching, burning or soreness\n• Pain, or sores\n\nThrush (thick, white, itchy) and BV (fishy smell) are common and treatable. STIs can cause similar symptoms, so it's best to get tested. Avoid douching or scented products, wear breathable cotton, and don't self-treat repeatedly.","period","stis,uti")
e("uti","Urinary tract infections","uti,urinary infection,burning when i pee,burning urine,pain when urinating,bladder infection,frequent urination,cystitis,blood in urine",
"Signs of a UTI: burning when you pee, needing to go often, cloudy or strong-smelling urine, lower tummy pain.\n\n• See a clinic. Antibiotics are usually needed\n• Drink plenty of water, and wipe front to back\n• Pee after sex\n\nGo urgently if you have fever, back or side pain, vomiting, or blood in the urine (kidney infection), or if you're pregnant.","health","discharge,stis")
e("stis","STIs and testing","sti,std,sexually transmitted,chlamydia,gonorrhoea,syphilis,herpes,trichomonas,sores genital,std test,sexual health test,sexual health,clinic test",
"Many STIs have no symptoms, so testing is the only way to know. Symptoms may include unusual discharge, pain when peeing, sores, itching, lower tummy pain or pain during sex.\n\n• Public clinics offer free STI and HIV testing and treatment\n• Most STIs are curable; others can be managed\n• Tell your partner(s) so they can be treated too\n• Condoms reduce risk\n\nA nurse will treat you without judgement.","health","hiv,contraception,hpv")
e("hiv","HIV, PrEP and PEP","hiv,aids,prep,pep,hiv test,hiv positive,arv,antiretroviral,u=u,undetectable,living with hiv,aids helpline",
"• Testing is free and confidential at public clinics\n• PEP: pills taken within 72 hours after possible exposure (e.g., assault or condom failure) to prevent HIV\n• PrEP: daily medicine that greatly lowers HIV risk, available at many clinics\n• Treatment (ART) allows people with HIV to live long, healthy lives. When the viral load is undetectable the virus can't be passed on sexually (U=U)\n• In pregnancy, treatment protects the baby\n\nNational AIDS Helpline: 0800 012 322 (free).","health","stis,afterassault,pregnant")
e("contraception","Contraception and birth control","contraception,birth control,the pill,injection,implant,iud,loop,condom,family planning,prevent pregnancy,depo,nuvaring,sterilisation,tubal ligation,which contraceptive",
"Common options at public clinics (free):\n• Condoms: protect against pregnancy and most STIs\n• Pill: daily; works if taken on time\n• Injection: every 2 or 3 months\n• Implant: lasts about 3 years\n• IUD (copper or hormonal): lasts several years\n• Permanent options: tubal ligation (for those sure they're done)\n\nA nurse can help you choose. Contraception doesn't protect you from STIs unless you also use a condom. You're allowed to choose, and nobody can force a method on you.","health","emergency_contraception,stis,pregnancy_test")
e("emergency_contraception","Emergency contraception","morning after pill,emergency contraception,plan b,after unprotected sex,condom broke,missed pill,took a risk,unprotected sex,levonorgestrel,ellaone",
"Emergency contraception can prevent pregnancy after unprotected sex or contraceptive failure.\n• Morning-after pills work best as early as possible, within 72 hours (some work up to 5 days)\n• A copper IUD fitted by a clinic within 5 days is the most effective option\n• Available free at public clinics and at pharmacies\n• It does not end an existing pregnancy\n\nIf you are worried about HIV, ask about PEP as well (within 72 hours). Take a pregnancy test if your period is late.","health","pregnancy_test,hiv,contraception")
e("hpv","Cervical screening, HPV and smears","pap smear,cervical cancer,cervical screening,hpv,smear test,hpv vaccine,abnormal pap,cervix,colposcopy",
"Cervical cancer is largely preventable.\n• Public clinics offer free Pap smears from age 30 (ask your clinic about the schedule, and sooner if you live with HIV)\n• The HPV vaccine is offered to girls in public schools, and protects against the virus that causes most cervical cancers\n• Condoms reduce risk, but don't remove it\n• Report bleeding after sex, between periods or after menopause\n\nRegular screening finds problems early, when they're easiest to treat. CANSA: 0800 22 66 22.","health","breast,stis")
e("breast","Breast health","breast cancer,breast lump,breast self exam,mammogram,breast pain,nipple discharge,lump in my breast,breast check,breast changes",
"Get to know how your breasts normally look and feel. See a clinic if you notice:\n• A new lump or thickened area\n• Dimpling, redness or skin changes\n• Nipple turning inward, or discharge (especially bloody)\n• Persistent one-sided pain\n\nMost lumps aren't cancer, but must be checked. Ask your clinic about mammograms, usually from around 40 or earlier if there's family history. CANSA: 0800 22 66 22.","health","hpv,stis")
e("menopause","Perimenopause and menopause","menopause,perimenopause,hot flushes,hot flashes,night sweats,period stopped age,vaginal dryness,hrt,change of life,mood changes 40s",
"Perimenopause (often from the 40s) brings irregular periods, hot flushes, night sweats, sleep problems, mood changes and vaginal dryness. Menopause is confirmed after 12 months without a period, on average around 51.\n\n• Regular exercise, sleep and less alcohol or caffeine can help\n• Treatments (including HRT) are available. Talk to a doctor\n• Keep up bone health and screening\n• Seek help for heavy bleeding or bleeding after menopause","health","late_period,depression,sleep")
e("anaemia","Iron and anaemia","anaemia,anemia,low iron,iron deficiency,always tired,pale,dizzy period,iron foods,ferritin",
"Heavy periods, pregnancy and low-iron diets can cause anaemia: tiredness, pale skin, dizziness, shortness of breath, headaches.\n\n• Eat iron-rich foods (red meat, liver, beans, lentils, dark leafy greens), with vitamin C (citrus, peppers)\n• Limit tea or coffee with meals, which lowers iron absorption\n• Clinics can test and provide iron tablets\n• Don't self-medicate with high iron doses without advice","health","heavy_period,nutrition,pregnant")
e("hygiene","Period products and hygiene","pads,tampons,menstrual cup,period underwear,toxic shock,tss,period hygiene,change pad,how often change tampon,sanitary,period products,cant afford pads",
"• Change pads or tampons every 4 to 8 hours. Never leave a tampon in for more than 8 hours\n• Menstrual cups can stay in up to 12 hours; sterilise between cycles\n• Use the lowest absorbency you need\n• Wash hands before and after, and avoid scented products\n• Toxic shock syndrome is rare but serious: sudden fever, rash, dizziness or vomiting while using a tampon means remove it and go to hospital\n• If you can't afford products, clinics, schools, NGOs and community programmes may help","period","heavy_period,cramps")
e("tracker_how","How the period tracker works","period tracker,track my period,predict period,cycle length,ovulation tracker,fertile window,cycle prediction,when is my next period,how accurate,period app",
"Log the first day of each period in HerHub, and I'll estimate your next period, ovulation and fertile window. More cycles make predictions more accurate.\n\n• Tap 'My period started today' or choose a date\n• Add flow, symptoms and notes in the daily log\n• Predictions are estimates, not medical advice or contraception\n\nWant me to open the Period tracker?","period","late_period,fertility")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Pregnancy and fertility
e("pregnancy_test","Am I pregnant? Testing","am i pregnant,pregnancy test,pregnant symptoms,signs of pregnancy,how soon test,positive test,home test,think im pregnant,could i be pregnant,early pregnancy signs",
"Early signs: missed period, nausea, tender breasts, tiredness, needing to pee often. Most home tests work from the first day of a missed period. Test in the morning and repeat after a few days if negative and your period hasn't come.\n\n• Clinics test for free and can confirm\n• If positive: go for antenatal care early; avoid alcohol and smoking; ask about folic acid\n• If you are unsure what to do, a counsellor can talk through all your options (see 'termination')\n\nIf you have bleeding with one-sided pain, seek urgent care.","preg","pregnant,termination,ectopic")
e("pregnant","Pregnancy care and antenatal visits","pregnant,pregnancy,antenatal,first trimester,second trimester,third trimester,due date,expecting,weeks pregnant,pregnancy advice,prenatal,baby coming,anc visit,mom to be",
"If you're pregnant:\n• Visit a clinic as early as you can for antenatal care and an HIV test. Keep every appointment\n• Folic acid is usually recommended before and early in pregnancy. Ask your clinic\n• Avoid alcohol and smoking, and ask a professional before taking any medicine\n• Eat varied, nourishing food, drink water, rest, and move gently\n• Use HerHub's Pregnancy tool for your due date and weekly progress\n\nGo urgently for bleeding, severe pain, severe headache, blurred vision, or if your baby moves much less.","preg","warning_pregnancy,nutrition,morning_sickness")
e("morning_sickness","Morning sickness and nausea","morning sickness,nausea pregnant,vomiting pregnant,hyperemesis,cant keep food down,sick in pregnancy,pregnancy nausea,ginger nausea",
"Nausea is common in early pregnancy and often eases after the first trimester.\n• Eat small, frequent snacks; keep crackers by the bed\n• Sip fluids, try ginger or cold foods, avoid triggers\n• Rest and fresh air help\n\nSee a clinic if you can't keep fluids down, are losing weight, are very dizzy, or have dark urine. That may be hyperemesis, which needs treatment.","preg","pregnant,warning_pregnancy")
e("warning_pregnancy","Pregnancy danger signs","pregnancy danger signs,bleeding in pregnancy,baby not moving,reduced movements,severe headache pregnant,preeclampsia,swollen feet pregnant,water broke,leaking fluid,contractions early,blurred vision pregnant,when to go to hospital pregnant",
"Go to a clinic or hospital urgently, or call 112, if you have:\n• Vaginal bleeding\n• Severe abdominal pain\n• Severe headache, blurred vision, sudden swelling of face or hands (possible pre-eclampsia)\n• Baby moving much less than usual\n• Fluid leaking or waters breaking, or regular contractions before 37 weeks\n• Fever or feeling very unwell\n\nDon't wait for a scheduled visit.","preg","pregnant,labour,miscarriage",1)
e("miscarriage","Miscarriage and pregnancy loss","miscarriage,lost my baby,pregnancy loss,stillbirth,bleeding and cramping pregnant,lost the pregnancy,passed a clot pregnant,baby died,grief after miscarriage",
"I'm so sorry. Pregnancy loss is painful, and grief is normal. It's not your fault.\n\n• Heavy bleeding, fever, severe pain or fainting: go to a hospital urgently\n• A clinic can confirm what's happening and help with care\n• Give yourself time to grieve; you may want to talk to someone. SADAG: 0800 21 22 23\n• Most people who miscarry go on to have healthy pregnancies, when they feel ready","preg","warning_pregnancy,depression,therapy")
e("ectopic","Ectopic pregnancy","ectopic,tubal pregnancy,one sided pain pregnant,shoulder tip pain,pain and bleeding early pregnancy",
"An ectopic pregnancy (outside the womb) is an emergency. Warning signs: one-sided lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, dizziness or fainting, pain at the tip of the shoulder.\n\nGo to a hospital immediately or call 112.","preg","warning_pregnancy,miscarriage",1)
e("termination","Termination of pregnancy","abortion,termination,terminate pregnancy,ctop,unwanted pregnancy,dont want to be pregnant,end the pregnancy,abortion clinic,legal abortion",
"In South Africa, the Choice on Termination of Pregnancy Act gives you the right to choose.\n• On request up to 12 weeks, at designated public facilities, free of charge\n• From 13 to 20 weeks under certain conditions (for example risk to health)\n• Nobody can force you. If you're under 18, you're encouraged (not required) to involve an adult\n• Pre- and post-abortion counselling is available\n• Don't use unregulated 'backstreet' providers or unsafe pills. They can be dangerous\n\nGo to a clinic as early as possible. Ask for a referral. You will be treated without judgement.","preg","pregnancy_test,contraception,depression")
e("labour","Labour and birth","labour,labor,contractions,when to go to hospital,giving birth,going into labour,birth plan,induction,c section,caesarean,epidural,water broke",
"Signs of labour: regular, strengthening contractions that get closer together, a 'show' of mucus or blood, or waters breaking.\n\n• Go to the clinic or hospital when contractions are about 5 minutes apart for an hour, your waters break, you have bleeding, or baby moves less\n• Pack your bag early: Road to Health card, ID, clothes for you and baby\n• You can ask questions and have a birth companion\n• Arrange transport in advance\n\nIf in doubt, call your clinic or 112.","preg","warning_pregnancy,newborn,pregnant")
e("breastfeeding","Breastfeeding","breastfeeding,breastfeed,milk supply,latch,sore nipples,engorged,expressing milk,formula or breast,mastitis,weaning",
"Breastfeeding is recommended exclusively for the first 6 months, if you can. Tips:\n• Feed on demand and make sure baby latches deeply\n• Sore nipples often improve with a better latch. Ask the nurse or lactation consultant\n• Red, hot, painful breast with fever may be mastitis. See a clinic\n• Living with HIV? Follow your treatment, as clinics give guidance on safe feeding\n• Fed is best. If you can't breastfeed, you haven't failed\n\nAsk your clinic about support groups.","preg","newborn,postnatal")
e("newborn","Newborn care","newborn,baby care,my baby,baby wont stop crying,baby fever,baby jaundice,cord care,road to health,immunisation,vaccination schedule,infant care,colic,baby sleep",
"Basics for newborns:\n• Keep your Road to Health booklet and attend immunisation visits\n• Keep baby warm, clean the cord stump dry, and always place baby to sleep on the back\n• Feed often, and watch wet nappies\n• Go to a clinic urgently if baby has a fever, is floppy or very sleepy, struggles to breathe, won't feed, or has yellow skin that spreads\n• Crying can be hard. If you feel overwhelmed, put baby down safely and take a breath. Never shake a baby\n\nChildline: 0800 055 555.","preg","breastfeeding,postnatal")
e("postnatal","Postnatal depression","postnatal depression,postpartum depression,baby blues,cant bond with baby,after birth sad,crying after birth,postpartum anxiety,i dont love my baby,overwhelmed new mom,postpartum",
"Many mothers feel tearful in the first two weeks (baby blues). If sadness, anxiety, numbness, guilt or thoughts of harming yourself or your baby last longer, it may be postnatal depression. It's common and treatable, and it isn't your fault.\n\n• Tell your clinic nurse or doctor\n• SADAG: 0800 21 22 23. Lifeline: 0861 322 322\n• Rest when you can, accept help, and talk to someone\n• If you have thoughts of harming yourself or your baby, call 0800 567 567 or 112 now","mind","depression,suicide,therapy",1)
e("fertility","Trying to conceive","fertility,trying to conceive,ttc,ovulation,fertile window,cant get pregnant,infertility,how to get pregnant,conceive,pregnant naturally,ovulate,when am i most fertile,ivf",
"To improve your chances:\n• Have sex every 1 to 2 days in your fertile window (the days before and on ovulation). HerHub estimates it from your cycle\n• Take folic acid, eat well, avoid smoking and alcohol, and keep a healthy weight\n• Most couples conceive within a year. If you're under 35 and haven't after 12 months (or 6 months if over 35), see a doctor\n• Irregular cycles, PCOS and STIs can affect fertility. Both partners can be tested\n\nOpen the Pregnancy & Fertility tool to see your window.","preg","pcos,tracker_how,pregnant")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- General wellbeing
e("nutrition","Healthy eating","nutrition,healthy eating,diet,what should i eat,vitamins,balanced diet,eat healthy,supplement,calcium,protein,weight,fruit and veg",
"Simple, nourishing habits:\n• Fill half your plate with vegetables and fruit, add protein and whole grains\n• Include iron (beans, greens, meat), calcium (milk, yoghurt, leafy greens) and healthy fats\n• Drink water through the day. HerHub can remind you\n• Limit sugary drinks and ultra-processed foods\n• Don't skip meals or follow extreme diets\n\nIf you're worried about eating or body image, you can talk to a counsellor.","health","water,anaemia,eatingdisorder")
e("water","Hydration","water,drink water,dehydrated,hydration,how much water,thirsty,glasses of water,water reminder",
"Most adults do well with around 6 to 8 glasses of fluid a day, more in heat, exercise, pregnancy or breastfeeding. Pale yellow urine is a good sign.\n\nLog your glasses in HerHub's Health & Wellness page, and turn on the Drink water reminder in Smart Notifications.","health","nutrition")
e("exercise","Exercise and movement","exercise,workout,movement,fitness,gym,walking for health,yoga,stretching,get active,period exercise,home workout",
"Aim for movement most days. Even a 20 to 30 minute walk helps your heart, mood and sleep.\n• Pick something you enjoy\n• Gentle exercise can ease period pain\n• Warm up, stay hydrated, and rest when needed\n• Walk or run in safe, well-lit places, or with a friend. Share your route using Safe Journey\n• Check with a doctor if you're pregnant, recovering or have a medical condition","health","sleep,walkhome")
e("sleep","Sleep and insomnia","sleep,tired,insomnia,cant sleep,sleep problems,trouble sleeping,nightmares,sleep routine,wake up at night,exhausted,sleepless",
"For better sleep:\n• Keep a regular bedtime and wake time\n• No screens for 30 minutes before bed, and keep the room cool and dark\n• Limit caffeine after midday, and heavy meals late\n• Try slow breathing or a body scan. HerHub has guided breathing\n• Write worries in a journal before bed\n\nIf poor sleep lasts weeks, or you have nightmares after trauma, see a healthcare professional.","mind","anxiety,exercise,trauma")
e("headache","Headaches and migraines","headache,migraine,head hurts,period headache,hormonal migraine",
"Many headaches ease with water, rest, a dark quiet room, and regular meals. Hormone changes can trigger migraines around periods.\n\nSee a doctor urgently for a sudden severe headache, headache with fever and stiff neck, vision changes, weakness, confusion, or a headache in pregnancy with swelling or blurred vision (possible pre-eclampsia).","health","warning_pregnancy,sleep")
e("firstaid","Basic first aid","first aid,burn,cut bleeding,faint,fainted,choking,seizure,allergic reaction,sprain,nosebleed,poison,dog bite",
"For emergencies call 112 or 10177. Basics until help arrives:\n• Bleeding: press firmly with a clean cloth\n• Burns: cool under running water for 20 minutes\n• Choking: encourage coughing; if it fails, give back blows then abdominal thrusts\n• Fainting: lay the person down and raise legs\n• Poisoning: call the Poisons Helpline 0861 555 777\n\nA first-aid course is a great skill to learn.","sos","emergency")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Mental health
e("anxiety","Anxiety and panic","anxious,anxiety,panic,panic attack,overwhelmed,cant cope,worried all the time,heart racing,racing thoughts,cant breathe anxious,nervous,on edge,stressed out,stress",
"That sounds really hard. Try this now:\n• Breathe in for 4, hold 4, out for 4, hold 4. Repeat a few rounds\n• Ground yourself: name 5 things you see, 4 you feel, 3 you hear, 2 you smell, 1 you taste\n• Remind yourself: 'This will pass. I am safe right now.'\n\nI can open guided breathing in HerHub. If anxiety keeps happening for weeks, talk to a clinic or SADAG (0800 21 22 23). Panic attacks are frightening but not dangerous.","mind","grounding,depression,sleep")
e("grounding","Grounding and calming exercises","grounding,calm down,calm myself,breathing exercise,relax,mindfulness,meditation,how to calm,5 4 3 2 1,box breathing,self soothe",
"A few quick ways to calm your body:\n• Box breathing: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4\n• 5-4-3-2-1 senses grounding\n• Press your feet into the floor, and notice the support beneath you\n• Splash cool water on your face\n• Name what you feel: 'I feel scared, and I'm safe right now'\n\nHerHub's Mental Health page has a guided breathing circle.","mind","anxiety,sleep")
e("depression","Sadness and depression","sad,depress,depressed,depression,lonely,hopeless,crying,low mood,empty,numb,no motivation,feel worthless,cant get out of bed,miserable",
"I'm sorry you're feeling this way. Your feelings are valid, and depression is a health condition, not a weakness.\n\n• Reach out to someone you trust today, even with a short message\n• Try one small kind thing: a shower, a walk, a meal\n• Keep a mood log in HerHub to spot patterns\n• If this has lasted more than two weeks, or you feel hopeless, please talk to a clinic or counsellor. SADAG: 0800 21 22 23\n• If you think about ending your life: 0800 567 567 or 112","mind","suicide,therapy,loneliness",1)
e("loneliness","Loneliness and isolation","loneliness,lonely,no friends,isolated,feel alone,nobody cares,miss someone,new in town,social isolation",
"Feeling lonely is human, and many people feel it. Small steps help:\n• Message one person today\n• Join a group around something you enjoy: sport, church, study, volunteering\n• Spend time outside, around other people\n• HerHub's Support & Community can connect you with others\n\nIf the loneliness feels heavy or hopeless, talk to someone: SADAG 0800 21 22 23 or Lifeline 0861 322 322.","support","depression,therapy")
e("trauma","Trauma and PTSD","trauma,ptsd,flashbacks,nightmares after,triggered,cant stop thinking about what happened,jumpy,scared after attack,after the attack,stress after assault,dissociate,numb after",
"After something frightening or violent, it's common to have flashbacks, nightmares, being jumpy, numbness, or avoiding reminders. These are normal reactions to abnormal events. They often ease with time, but support helps.\n\n• You didn't cause it, and you're not weak\n• Talk to a trauma counsellor, doctor or support group\n• Grounding and breathing can help in the moment\n• GBV Command Centre: 0800 428 428. SADAG: 0800 21 22 23\n\nIf symptoms last more than a month or you feel unsafe, get professional help.","mind","grounding,therapy,sleep")
e("therapy","Finding counselling or therapy","therapy,counselling,psychologist,counsellor,therapist,mental health help,where to get therapy,afford therapy,free counselling,see someone about my feelings,psychiatrist",
"Options in South Africa:\n• SADAG: 0800 21 22 23 (referrals to affordable help)\n• Public clinics and community health centres: ask for a mental health nurse or referral\n• Lifeline: 0861 322 322 (counselling)\n• Universities and employers often provide counselling\n• Private psychologists. Some medical aids cover sessions\n\nYou don't need to be in crisis to ask for support.","mind","mentalhelp,depression")
e("burnout","Burnout and stress","burnout,burned out,work stress,exhausted at work,overworked,too much pressure,cant keep up,study stress,exam stress,caregiver fatigue",
"Burnout shows up as exhaustion, cynicism and feeling ineffective. What helps:\n• Rest, boundaries and saying no to extra tasks\n• Break big jobs into small steps\n• Move your body and spend time with people who lift you\n• Talk to a manager, lecturer or counsellor about your load\n• Short breathing breaks during the day\n\nIf it lasts or affects your health, see a professional.","mind","anxiety,sleep")
e("grief","Grief and loss","grief,grieving,bereavement,someone died,lost my mother,lost a loved one,death in the family,mourning,funeral",
"I'm so sorry for your loss. Grief has no timetable, and it can bring sadness, anger, numbness and waves of emotion.\n• Let yourself feel, and talk about the person\n• Keep to simple routines: food, water, sleep\n• Lean on family, friends or your community. Faith or support groups can help\n• Reach out for counselling if grief feels stuck or unbearable: SADAG 0800 21 22 23","mind","depression,therapy")
e("selfesteem","Self-esteem and body image","self esteem,self-esteem,i hate myself,body image,ugly,fat,not good enough,confidence,insecure,imposter,compare myself,hate my body",
"You are worthy as you are. Some ideas:\n• Notice self-criticism and respond as you would to a friend\n• Limit social media that makes you feel less than\n• List three things your body did for you today\n• Move and eat for health and joy, not punishment\n• Talk to someone you trust or a counsellor\n\nHerHub's daily affirmations are in the Mental Health page.","mind","eatingdisorder,anxiety,therapy")
e("eatingdisorder","Eating disorders","eating disorder,anorexia,bulimia,binge eating,starving myself,purging,afraid of food,obsessed with weight,laxatives for weight,skip meals to lose weight",
"If food, weight or your body is taking over your thoughts, you deserve support. Eating disorders are real and treatable, and they can affect anyone.\n\n• Speak to a doctor, clinic or counsellor. SADAG can refer you: 0800 21 22 23\n• Tell someone you trust\n• Try not to weigh yourself or avoid food as punishment\n• Seek urgent help if you feel faint, have chest pain, or think about harming yourself\n\nRecovery is possible.","mind","selfesteem,therapy")
e("anger","Anger and relationships","anger,i get angry,lose my temper,fight with partner,arguing,conflict,toxic friend,family problems,mother in law,relationship advice,breakup,heartbreak,he cheated",
"Strong feelings are part of relationships. Helpful steps:\n• Pause, breathe, and step away before reacting\n• Use 'I feel...' statements and listen to the other person\n• Healthy relationships have respect, trust and safety. Fear, control or violence is not normal\n• After a breakup, give yourself time to grieve. Talk to friends or a counsellor\n\nIf you feel afraid of your partner, see the abuse information, or call 0800 428 428.","mind","signs_abuse,depression,grief")
e("substance","Alcohol and drugs","alcohol,drinking too much,drugs,addiction,substance abuse,rehab,drinking problem,smoking,weed,i cant stop drinking,partner drinks,sanca",
"If alcohol or drugs are affecting your life, help is available without judgement.\n• SADAG substance abuse line: 0800 12 13 14\n• Clinics can refer you for treatment, and many areas have support groups\n• If someone you love drinks and becomes violent, your safety comes first. See GBV help, 0800 428 428\n• Never drink and drive, and watch your drink when out\n\nSmall steps count.","mind","gbvhelp,nightout,therapy")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Finance and digital
e("scam","Spotting scams","scam,fraud,phishing,sms from bank,suspicious message,fake link,is this a scam,spam,smishing,fake website,suspicious email,too good to be true,fake bank,whatsapp scam",
"Scam red flags: urgency or threats, requests for your OTP, PIN or password, links to unfamiliar sites, strange sender numbers, prizes you didn't enter for, and offers that are too good to be true.\n\n• Banks will never ask for your PIN, password or OTP\n• Don't click links. Type the website yourself\n• Call your bank on the number on your card\n• Paste the suspicious message here and I'll check it for you\n• Report to your bank, and to the FSCA: 0800 20 3722","finance","otp,simswap,scam_after")
e("otp","OTP and PIN safety","otp,one time pin,pin,share my pin,bank asked for otp,verification code,cvv,someone asked for my code,never share otp",
"Never share your OTP, PIN, password, CVV or verification code, not even with someone who says they are from your bank, the police or a delivery company. They are the keys to your money and accounts.\n\nIf you shared one by mistake, call your bank immediately on the number on your card, block the card, and change your passwords.","finance","scam,scam_after")
e("simswap","SIM swap and account takeover","sim swap,simswap,lost signal suddenly,phone lost network,account takeover,whatsapp hijacked,someone took my number,sim card replaced,hacked bank",
"A SIM swap is when criminals move your number to a new SIM to receive your OTPs. Warning signs: your phone suddenly loses network for no reason, and you get messages about SIM or account changes.\n\n• Call your mobile provider and bank immediately from another phone\n• Ask them to block your SIM and review recent transactions\n• Change passwords and turn on two-factor authentication using an authenticator app\n• Report to the police and keep a case number","finance","scam,passwords,scam_after")
e("scam_after","I've been scammed: what to do","i was scammed,i got scammed,lost money to a scam,scammed me,i clicked the link,gave them my details,sent money to a scammer,stolen money,fraudulent transaction,unauthorised transaction,they took my money",
"Act quickly:\n1. Call your bank on the number on your card, and ask them to block your cards and stop transactions\n2. Change passwords for banking, email and social media\n3. Contact your mobile provider if a SIM swap is suspected\n4. Report to the police and get a case number\n5. Report to the FSCA (0800 20 3722). You may also lodge a complaint with the Ombudsman for Banking Services (0860 800 900)\n6. Keep evidence: screenshots, messages and receipts\n\nIt's not your fault. Scammers are professionals.","finance","scam,simswap,legalaid")
e("romance_scam","Romance and investment scams","romance scam,love scam,asked me for money,online boyfriend money,investment scheme,ponzi,pyramid,crypto scam,forex scam,doubled my money,stokvel scam,job scam,advance fee",
"Common patterns: someone you've never met builds a quick, deep bond, then has an emergency needing money; or promises huge, guaranteed returns.\n• Never send money, gift cards or crypto to someone you haven't met in person\n• Check financial providers are registered with the FSCA\n• If it sounds too good to be true, it is\n• Talk to someone you trust before sending money\n\nIf you've lost money: see 'I've been scammed'. FSCA: 0800 20 3722.","finance","scam_after,onlinedating,scam")
e("loansharks","Loan sharks and debt","loan shark,mashonisa,debt,in debt,owe money,garnishee,blacklisted,credit,loan,cant pay my loan,debt collector,payday loan",
"If you're in debt:\n• Use registered credit providers (check with the National Credit Regulator). Unregistered lenders (mashonisas) often charge illegal interest and may hold your card or ID. That's illegal\n• Never give away your bank card or PIN\n• A debt counsellor can help you restructure repayments\n• Know your rights: debt collectors may not threaten or harass you\n• Legal Aid: 0800 110 110\n\nAsk for help before things spiral.","finance","budget,economic_abuse,legalaid")
e("budget","Budgeting and saving","budget,budgeting,saving money,savings,save,emergency fund,money management,stokvel,how to save,pay myself first,financial independence,start saving",
"A simple plan:\n• List your income and must-pay costs first (housing, food, transport)\n• Try 50/30/20: needs, wants, savings. Adjust to your situation\n• Start a small emergency fund, even R50 a week. It builds safety\n• Use a stokvel or savings account with no easy withdrawals\n• Track spending for a month\n\nHerHub's Financial Safety page has a savings goal tracker. Want me to open it?","finance","economic_abuse,scam,loansharks")
e("passwords","Passwords and account security","password,passwords,two factor,2fa,strong password,account security,hacked,change password,secure my account,authenticator,social media privacy,privacy settings,delete my data",
"Protect your accounts:\n• Use long, unique passwords (a password manager helps) and don't reuse them\n• Turn on two-factor authentication, using an app where possible\n• Review privacy settings, and who can see your location and photos\n• Log out of old devices and check 'linked devices'\n• Lock your phone with a PIN or biometric, and hide notification previews\n• If someone controlling or abusive knows your passwords, change them from a safe device\n\nSee HerHub's Digital & Personal Security.","security","cyberstalk,simswap")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- App help
e("app_sos","How does SOS work?","how does sos work,what does sos do,sos button,use sos,how to use sos,emergency button,panic",
"Tap SOS on Home to open Personal Safety & Emergency. From there you can:\n• Call 112, Police 10111 or Ambulance 10177\n• Alert your Trusted Circle with an SMS and map link\n• Sound the loud siren, with 10 flashlight blinks\n• Follow the 'If you feel unsafe' steps\n\nHerHub doesn't dispatch emergency services itself. Always call them directly in danger.","sos","handsfree,circle,sirenhelp")
e("handsfree","Hands-free SOS (power button)","hands free,hands-free,power button,double press,press power twice,sos without unlocking,screen off sos,silent sos",
"Hands-free SOS lets you trigger help without opening the app: press the power button twice quickly, even with the screen off. You get a 5-second chance to cancel, then HerHub texts your Trusted Circle your location, and can sound the siren and call your first contact.\n\nTurn it on in Personal Safety & Emergency. Set HerHub to 'Unrestricted' battery use so Android keeps it running, and test it. Don't rely on it alone.","sos","app_sos,circle,sirenhelp")
e("circle","Trusted Circle","trusted circle,trusted contacts,add contact,emergency contact,who gets my location,add my mom,add a friend,remove contact,sms alert contacts",
"Your Trusted Circle is the people who receive your alerts. Add at least one person: name, phone number and relationship. When you tap 'Alert my trusted circle', or when SOS fires, HerHub sends them an SMS with your location link.\n\n• Choose people who will answer and can reach you\n• Tell them they're on your circle, so they know what to do\n• Keep numbers up to date\n\nWant me to open Trusted Circle?","circle","app_sos,journey_how")
e("journey_how","Safe Journey","safe journey,journey timer,check in timer,arrival check in,how does safe journey work,trip timer,start a journey,i arrived safely",
"Safe Journey is a trip timer with a safety check-in:\n1. Enter your destination and how many minutes you expect to take\n2. HerHub reminds you to check in when time is up\n3. Tap 'I've arrived safely', or alert your circle if something is wrong\n4. You can add 15 minutes if you're delayed\n\nUse it for walks home, rides, dates and trips. Want me to open it?","journey","circle,walkhome")
e("map_how","Safety Map","safety map,map,report an incident,community report,nearby help,hospital near me,police near me,safe places,danger zone,map how to",
"The Safety Map shows your location and community safety notices. You can:\n• Tap Me to centre on your position\n• Report an incident to warn others\n• Tap Help to find nearby police, hospitals and clinics\n\nMaps are © OpenStreetMap contributors. Reports are community information, not verified by emergency services.","map","app_sos,walkhome")
e("privacy_app","Your privacy in HerHub","privacy,is my data safe,who sees my data,delete my data,data stored,guest mode,account backup,sign out,delete account,private log,quick exit,hide app",
"• Without an account, your data stays on this phone\n• With an account, your data backs up to your private account space\n• Your period, mood and journal entries are never shown to others\n• Use Quick Exit in Abuse & Harassment Support to close the app fast\n• You can clear data or delete your account in Digital & Personal Security\n• Hide notification previews on your lock screen if someone else may see your phone\n\nHerHub is a support tool, not emergency services.","security","passwords,cyberstalk")
e("notifications_how","Reminders and notifications","reminder,reminders,notifications,notify me,remind me,pill reminder,water reminder,daily reminder,medication reminder,turn off reminder",
"In Smart Notifications you can switch on daily reminders for water, medication, mood check-ins, safety check-ins and logging your period, and pick the time for each. If reminders don't appear, allow notifications for HerHub in your phone's settings.","notify","water,tracker_how")
e("appearance","Dark mode and appearance","dark mode,light mode,change theme,night mode,appearance,change colour,dark theme",
"Go to More > Appearance and choose Light, Dark or Auto (follows your phone). All HerHub screens and icons adapt automatically.","","privacy_app")
e("about_ai","What can you do?","what can you do,who are you,what are you,help me,how do you work,are you real,are you a robot,are you ai,your name,what can i ask,commands,what do you know",
"I'm your HerHub guide. I run privately on your phone and don't send your chats anywhere.\n\nAsk me about:\n• Safety: walking home, rides, dates, drink spiking, stalking\n• Abuse and GBV: protection orders, leaving safely, helplines\n• Health: periods, pregnancy, contraception, STIs, menopause\n• Mind: anxiety, sadness, trauma, sleep\n• Money: scams, OTPs, budgets, debt\n• HerHub features: SOS, siren, Trusted Circle, Safe Journey\n\nI'm not a doctor, lawyer or emergency service. In danger, call 112 or 10111.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Small talk
e("hello","Say hello","hello,hi,hey,howzit,good morning,good afternoon,good evening,sawubona,molo,heita,hallo,yo",
"Hi, I'm your HerHub guide. I can give general safety, health and wellbeing guidance, and open the right tool for you. What's on your mind?","","about_ai")
e("thanks","Thank you","thank you,thanks,thanks a lot,thx,appreciate it,ngiyabonga,enkosi,dankie,that helps,helpful",
"You're welcome. I'm glad I could help. You're not alone, and I'm here whenever you need me.","","about_ai")
e("bye","Goodbye","bye,goodbye,see you,talk later,cheers,goodnight,good night,ok bye",
"Take care of yourself. Remember: your safety comes first, and help is always one tap away in HerHub.","")
e("howareyou","How are you?","how are you,how are you doing,how r u,hows it going,you ok",
"I'm here and ready to help. How are you feeling today? You can tell me, or tap one of the topics below.","mind","anxiety,depression,about_ai")
e("joke","Something light","joke,tell me a joke,make me laugh,cheer me up,funny,i need a laugh,lighten the mood",
"Here's a little smile: Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field.\n\nIf you're really struggling and not just needing a laugh, I'm here for that too.","mind","grounding,depression")
e("affirm","Encouragement","affirmation,encourage me,motivate me,i need motivation,say something nice,positive words,pep talk,inspire me,i need hope,give me strength",
"You are allowed to take up space. Your feelings are valid, and they will change. Asking for help is strength, and one small step is still progress. You deserve safety, rest and kindness, today and every day.","mind","grounding,anxiety")
